# Line-ending policy: every platform checks out the same bytes.
#
# The fitting pipeline is byte-oriented: BNGL templates are materialized by
# token substitution and fed to the engine, conf and exp files are parsed
# line-wise, and the app inlines js/css assets verbatim into exported
# reports. Windows Git's default CRLF conversion changes those bytes at
# checkout (found by the Windows CI job as a byte-identity test failure),
# so text checks out LF everywhere, with the two Windows launcher scripts
# as the deliberate exceptions.
* text=auto eol=lf
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.ps1 text eol=crlf

# The engine's input formats, pinned by name rather than left to the
# `text=auto` heuristic above. `auto` asks git to GUESS whether a blob is
# text; a template that ever picked up a byte git read as binary would drop
# out of the policy silently, and the way you would find that out is a fit
# behaving differently on one platform. These are the formats BNG2.pl and
# PyBNF parse, so they say so rather than inferring it. Only .bngl and
# .conf are tracked today (flubnf/templates); .exp is written at run time
# and is listed so a committed fixture would land under the same rule.
*.bngl text eol=lf
*.exp  text eol=lf
*.conf text eol=lf

# The generated site. site.json IS the review, so it stays a normal diffable
# text file; the vendored Plotly bundle beside it is 4.9 MB of upstream
# minified JS that would bury every one of those reviews, so it is excluded
# from diffs and from language statistics without being excluded from the
# repository -- the page needs it to work offline.
site/plotly.min.js -diff linguist-vendored
